CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets. The term Cascading refers to the algorithm or set of rules which are used by browser to decide the flow of styling down 
the HTML elements when styling from multiple sources is being applied on them. 
The styling applied on an element is decided on the basis of three rueles,

> Source of styling and importance: The user applied styling overrides the browser's built-in styling for elements and the stylings marked with !important will 
override all the other user's defined styling as well. This ignores all the prescedence order of selectors, source or order and force to apply the styling.
Ex: h2{
    color: red !important;
    }

> Specificity: If the styling is coming from the same source(like user defined CSS), then the precedence is determined by the selecters type. Different selectors 
target the elements more or less specifically.
Specificity order:
!important keyword > inline CSS > ID selectors > Class selectors > Elements selectors > Universal selector. 

> Styling order: If two styling have same origins and specificity, then the order in which they are written determines the final styling. The styling written last
in the code overrides the styling written before for the same element. 
